## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design multi-tenant throughput controls with backpressure and strong delivery guarantees in KCNA.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation
- Token bucket rate limiting
- Backpressure signaling to producers
- Idempotent dedupe for at-least-once
- Quota-based configurability and Canary testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for per-tenant token bucket limiter
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test under burst traffic across tenants and ensure fairness?
- How would you evolve this to multi-broker coordination with dynamic quota changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical TTL-state reasoning and side-input-driven re-enrichment under at-least-once guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- TTL cache per merchant
- Side-input rule versioning
- Exactly-once-like semantics via idempotent emits and commitOffsets
- Re-enrichment on rule updates

## Code Example
```javascript
function enrich(txn, ctx) {
  const rule = cache.get(txn.merchantId);
  if (!rule || rule.expiry < now()) {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: null, ruleVersion: rule?.ruleVersion ?? 0 });
  } else {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: rule.riskScore, ruleVersion: rule.ruleVersion });
  }
}
```

